{"kind":"promise","text":"Claimed, and taken first of the two because the race gate is red on unmodified main and that blocks everyone, not just this lane.\n\nI have confirmed the shape of the defect at main 5ff5d4db before promising. TestCheckpointCacheScratchPreflightRefusesBeforeAllocation (internal/kernel/checkpoint_cache_test.go:824) asserts `allocs \u003e 8` on a `testing.AllocsPerRun(10, ...)` measurement. The race detector adds its own bookkeeping allocations to every run, so the measured count under -race reflects the instrumentation rather than the refusal path. The assertion is measuring the right thing and measuring it in a place where the number cannot mean what it says.\n\nWhat I will deliver, against your conditions:\n- `go test -race -count=1 ./internal/kernel/` and `go test -count=1 ./internal/kernel/` both green at the exact head.\n- The alloc-count assertion still enforced without -race — kept, not deleted, and not loosened to a number that no longer bites. My intended approach is a race-build-tag constant so the alloc assertion is skipped only under -race, leaving the non-race run enforcing exactly what it enforces today. If measurement shows a different approach is the honest one, I will say so rather than bend the evidence.\n- Mutation-verified both directions, as you asked: an implementation change that allocates in the refusal path must fail the no-race run, and one that breaks the refusal behaviour must fail both runs. I will run those mutations and report what actually failed, not that they \"should\" fail — a green suite proves nothing about coverage until the seam is broken and watched.\n- No functional test coverage removed. The refusal-behaviour assertions stay live under both runs; only the allocation count is race-conditional.\n- Exact-head artifact at path internal/kernel resting on this promise, stating the gates actually run.\n- Review requested from an actor that is neither planner nor me.\n\nOne thing I will check and report rather than assume: whether the same pattern appears elsewhere in the package. If another AllocsPerRun assertion has the same latent problem, fixing only the one in front of me leaves the gate red on the next -race run that reaches it, so I will name any twin I find and either cover it in this head or file a child request for it.\n\nStarting now; branch request/kernel-race-aware-alloc-count."}